---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import gaz from "../data/gazetteer.json";

const FAM = [
  ["papic", "Papić", "Two villages and nothing else"],
  ["prpic", "Prpić", "One village, three houses"],
  ["zubrinic", "Žubrinić", "One town, then one port"],
  ["blazevic", "Blažević", "Twenty places"],
];
const places = [
  ["Otočac", 69, "1812–1888", "The Gacka valley. The regimental town, and the core of the name.", "/frontier"],
  ["Australia", 35, "1885–1999", "Port Pirie, Port Germein, Solomontown, Quorn, Broken Hill, Whyalla, Ardrossan, Adelaide.", "/port-pirie"],
  ["Prozor", 8, "1858–1936", "Five km south-east of Otočac, on Roman Arupium. Houses 50 and 245.", null],
  ["Senj", 7, "1849–1886", "Over the Velebit. Five of the seven records are deaths.", "/senj"],
  ["Baška", 2, "1818", "On the island of Krk — and unexplained.", null],
  ["Šumećica", 1, "1768", "House 2. The oldest dated Žubrinić record in the archive.", "/frontier"],
];
const decades = [
  [1760, 1, 0], [1810, 3, 0], [1830, 10, 0], [1840, 33, 0], [1850, 18, 0],
  [1860, 8, 0], [1870, 6, 0], [1880, 4, 7], [1890, 0, 8], [1900, 0, 4],
  [1920, 0, 4], [1930, 1, 1], [1950, 0, 1], [1960, 0, 1], [1970, 0, 2],
  [1980, 0, 5], [1990, 0, 2],
];
const max = 33;
---
<Base title="The Map"
  eyebrow="All four surnames, read as a place rather than a pedigree"
  dek="Every town the name appears in, what the records there consist of, and the two things the shape of the data gives away about the people who collected it.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Only <strong>122</strong> of the 232 Žubrinić records name a place at all, and
    they fall into <strong>six</strong> of them. Frontier registers identify a household by
    <strong>village and house number</strong> rather than by surname, so the house numbers are kept
    here: they are the closest thing these records have to a family identifier.</p>
    <table class="reg" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <thead><tr><th>Place</th><th>Records</th><th>Span</th><th>What is there</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{places.map(([n, c, s, d, href]) => (
        <tr><td><strong>{href ? <a href={u(href)}>{n}</a> : n}</strong></td>
          <td class="mono">{c}</td><td class="mono">{s}</td><td class="dim">{d}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The first thing the shape gives away</div>
    <h2>The Croatian record stops in 1888</h2>
    <p class="lede">Set the Croatian records against the Australian ones by decade and the two
    curves cross once, in the <strong>1880s</strong> — and then the Croatian side simply
    <strong>ends</strong>.</p>
    <table class="reg" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <thead><tr><th>Decade</th><th>Croatia</th><th>Australia</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{decades.map(([d, cro, aus]) => (
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">{d}s</td>
          <td><span class="bar cro" style={`width:${(cro / max) * 76}%`}></span>
            <span class="mono dim">{cro || ""}</span></td>
          <td><span class="bar aus" style={`width:${(aus / max) * 76}%`}></span>
            <span class="mono dim">{aus || ""}</span></td>
        </tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>This is not the family leaving Croatia.</strong>
    One man left. <strong>Forebears counts 292 Žubrinići living in Croatia today</strong>, a quarter
    of them in Lika-Senj — the same county as Otočac. They are still there. What ended in 1888 is
    <em>the research</em>: it followed the emigrant to Port Pirie and stopped following everybody who
    stayed. <a href={u("/name")}>The distribution →</a></div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The second thing</div>
    <h2>A hundred births and seven deaths</h2>
    <p class="lede">The Croatian records are <strong>almost entirely baptisms</strong>. Across the
    whole surname there are about a hundred dated Croatian births and
    <strong>seven</strong> recorded deaths — and <strong>thirty-three of the births fall in the
    1840s alone</strong>.</p>
    <p>That is not what a family looks like. It is what <em>one decade of one register, read hard
    and then abandoned</em>, looks like. The Otočac death books have barely been opened.</p>
    <h3 style="margin-top:1.6rem">The seven deaths, in full</h3>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Name</th><th>Place</th><th>Age</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="mono">1849</td><td>Mihovil Žubrinić</td><td>Senj</td><td class="mono">47</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">1861</td><td>Nicolaus Žubrinić</td><td>Senj</td><td class="mono">8</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">1861</td><td>Mathias Žubrinić</td><td>Senj</td><td class="mono">1</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">1863</td><td>Joannes Žubrinić</td><td>Senj</td><td class="mono">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">1871</td><td>Lucca Žubrinić <span class="dim">— Nicolaus's sister</span></td><td>Otočac</td><td class="mono">18</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">1873</td><td>Georgius Žubrinić</td><td>Senj</td><td class="mono">55</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">1936</td><td>Kata Žubrinić</td><td>Prozor</td><td class="mono">76</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem">Five of the seven are at <strong>Senj</strong>, and three
    of those five are <strong>children</strong> — aged eight, one and nought, in 1861, 1861 and 1863.
    They are from three <em>different</em> households. Whether that is a hospital, an epidemic, or
    simply where the town's register happens to have been read is not something this archive can
    say.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">What the baptisms know that the tree does not</div>
    <h2>Five deaths nobody wrote down</h2>
    <p class="lede">A Catholic family of this period <strong>reused a given name when the child who
    carried it had died</strong>. So a sibling set holding two Franciscas is, by itself, a record of
    a death — even where no death entry has ever been read.</p>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>1828 → 1836</th><td>Michael Žubrinić &amp; Maria — <strong>Josephus</strong> twice, eight years apart</td></tr>
      <tr><th>1834 → 1844</th><td>Elias Žubrinić &amp; <a href={u("/frontier")}>Ana Untersinger</a> — <strong>Maria</strong> twice, ten years apart</td></tr>
      <tr><th>1841 → 1845</th><td>Philippus Žubrinić &amp; Maria Begović — <strong>Stephanus</strong> twice, four years apart</td></tr>
      <tr><th>1844 → 1852</th><td>Joannes Žubrinić &amp; Maria Kostelac — <strong>Francisca</strong> twice, eight years apart</td></tr>
      <tr><th>1846 → 1848</th><td>the same couple again — <strong>Joannes</strong> twice, two years apart</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">One household, that of <strong>Joannes Žubrinić and Maria
    Kostelac</strong>, shows it twice: eight recorded children between 1844 and 1853, of whom
    <strong>two names are used again</strong> — and their son Nicolaus, born 1853, is one of the
    three children who die at Senj. Of eight births, three deaths, and only one of the three
    written down anywhere.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>Stated as an inference.</strong> Name reuse is a strong convention, not
    a law: a name can be repeated for a living godparent's sake, and a register can be miscopied.
    But five instances in one surname, all with gaps of two to ten years, is a pattern.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The same test, run on all four families</div>
    <h2>Where the deaths were written down, and where they were not</h2>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Families with a child</th><th>Deaths implied by reuse</th>
        <th>Same name, same year</th><th>Impossible gaps</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><a href={u("/zubrinic")}>Žubrinić</a></td><td class="mono">61</td>
          <td class="mono">5</td><td class="mono">3</td><td class="mono">1</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href={u("/blazevic")}>Blažević</a></td><td class="mono">63</td>
          <td class="mono">2</td><td class="mono">2</td><td class="mono">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href={u("/prpic")}>Prpić</a></td><td class="mono">29</td>
          <td class="mono">2</td><td class="mono">1</td><td class="mono">1</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href={u("/papic")}>Papić</a></td><td class="mono">39</td>
          <td class="mono"><strong>0</strong></td><td class="mono">1</td><td class="mono">0</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.8rem"><strong>Re-run 10 September 2026</strong>, after four
    sorting buckets were found being published as ancestors and removed. <strong>Every figure above
    is unchanged</strong> — the buckets were already excluded by this tool, which is why it
    reported them as excluded and the site build did not. The Blažević family count, left blank
    when this table was first drawn, is <strong>63</strong>.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>The zero is the interesting number.</strong> It does not
    mean the Papići lost no children. It means <strong>their losses were written down</strong>:
    Milka's sister <strong>Zora died in 1899, the year she was born</strong>, and her brother
    <strong>Stanko in 1903, aged one</strong> — both recorded as deaths, not merely implied by a
    reused name.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">The Papići are a <strong>Senj</strong> family, and somebody read the
    Senj death register. The Blaževići and Žubrinići are village families whose death books have
    barely been opened — so their infant deaths survive only as a name used twice. The pattern in
    this table is a map of <strong>where the research went</strong>, not of where children died.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>And one impossibility worth naming.</strong> The Prpić scan turns up
    <em>Juraj</em> entered twice in one sibling set — <strong>1862 and 1962</strong>, a hundred years
    apart. Like the Žubrinić pair forty-six years apart, that is not a reused name; it is a record
    attached to the wrong family. <a href={u("/method")}>Method →</a></div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>And it catches an error on its own</h2>
    <p>The same scan turned up a sixth repeated name that is <strong>not</strong> a reused one:
    <strong>Nicolaus (1834) and Nikola (1880)</strong>, entered as brothers,
    <strong>forty-six years apart</strong>. No mother bears both. It is the same faulty attachment
    that put a woman born in <strong>1835</strong> forward as the mother of a son born in
    <strong>1834</strong>. <a href={u("/frontier")}>The correction →</a></p>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem">Three further pairs share a name <em>and</em> a birth
    year — Catharina 1840 twice, Josephus 1845 twice, and Mihovil 1802 beside Michael 1811 with both
    deaths recorded. Those are almost certainly <strong>one child entered twice</strong>, from two
    passes over the same register under two spellings of the name.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Four surnames, four different shapes</h2>
    <p class="lede">Run the same reduction over each name and they do not look alike at all. One
    sits in two villages; one sits in three houses of a single village; one leaves for a single
    port on the other side of the world; one is simply everywhere. A surname's map is a fact about
    the family, and these four maps are four different kinds of family.</p>

    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      {FAM.map(([k, label, gloss]) => {
        const g = gaz[k];
        const top = g.places.filter((p) => p.n > 1).slice(0, 5);
        return (
          <div class="card">
            <h3>{label} <span class="dim">— {gloss}</span></h3>
            <p class="dek" style="margin:.3rem 0 .7rem">
              <strong>{g.records}</strong> records · <strong>{g.placed}</strong> name a place ·
              <strong> {g.places.length}</strong> distinct places</p>
            <table class="reg">
              <tbody>{top.map((pl) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><a href={u("/people/?q=" + encodeURIComponent(pl.village))}>{pl.village}</a>
                    {pl.houses.length > 0 &&
                      <span class="dim"> · {pl.houses.length === 1 ? "house" : "houses"} {pl.houses.join(", ")}</span>}
                  </td>
                  <td class="mono">{pl.n}</td>
                  <td class="mono dim">{pl.from ? `${pl.from}–${pl.to}` : "—"}</td>
                </tr>))}</tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        );
      })}
    </div>

    <div class="qa" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <h3>What the four shapes say</h3>
      <p><strong>Papić is the tightest name in this archive.</strong> Ninety-five of its ninety-six
      placed records fall in <strong>two</strong> villages — <strong>Selce</strong>, running
      1793–1880, and <strong>Senj</strong>, running 1857–1981. The two spans overlap by
      twenty-three years and then Selce stops. That is not two families; it is
      <a href={u("/senj")}>one family moving into the town</a>, and the
      <a href={u("/name")}>spelling moves with them</a>.</p>

      <p><strong>Prpić is a village with house numbers.</strong> Fifty-two records at
      <strong>Krivi Put</strong>, in houses <span class="mono">113</span>,
      <span class="mono">129</span> and <span class="mono">136</span> — and Antonija, Hedviga's
      great-grandmother, is born at <a href={u("/people/?q=Krivi%20Put")}>Krivi Put 136</a>. A
      Frontier village identifies a household by its number, so those three numbers are the closest
      thing here to three family branches.</p>

      <p><strong>Žubrinić is a town and a port.</strong> Sixty-eight at <strong>Otočac</strong>
      ending in <strong>1888</strong>, twenty-five at <strong>Port Pirie</strong> beginning in
      <strong>1885</strong>. The two barely overlap: the Croatian record stops almost exactly where
      the Australian one starts, which is what emigration looks like in a register.
      <a href={u("/port-pirie")}>Port Pirie →</a></p>

      <p><strong>Blažević is everywhere, and that is the point.</strong> Twenty distinct places
      against Papić's three. It is the <a href={u("/name")}>seventeenth commonest surname in
      Croatia</a>, and a common name in a small district produces exactly this: Mrzli Dol,
      Senj, Krivi Put, Smokvica Krmpotska, Krmpote, Sv. Jakov, Krmpotske Vodice — seven places
      inside twenty miles. Which is why <a href={u("/blazevic")}>house 114 at Smokvica</a> had to
      be settled by a house number and not by a surname.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Addresses the tree does not hold</div>
    <h2>Two houses that only a daughter's wedding records</h2>
    <p class="lede">Everything above is reduced from the tree. But a marriage entry names the
    bride's parents, their occupation <em>and their house</em> — so a woman's wedding is often the
    only place a dead mother's maiden name and address were ever written down. Reading eleven
    collateral marriages produced <strong>nine</strong> addresses that appear nowhere in the export.</p>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>Address</th><th>Family</th><th>How it surfaced</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Poljice kbr. 38</strong><br /><span class="dim">župa Otočac</span></td>
          <td><strong>Žubrinić</strong></td>
          <td>Katica Gomerčić's marriage, 24 July 1911. Her mother is entered
            <strong>†Marija rodj. Žubrinić</strong>, <em>poljodjelka</em> of Poljice 38. The
            gazetteer above knows the Otočac Žubrinići at Otočac, Prozor and Šumećica — not here.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Mrzli Dol kbr. 2 and kbr. 4</strong><br />
            <strong>Krivi Put kbr. 23, 46 and 51</strong><br />
            <strong>Alan kbr. 51 and kbr. 54</strong><br /><span class="dim">župa Krivi Put</span></td>
          <td><strong>Prpić</strong>, Rončević,<br />Tomljanović, Vukelić</td>
          <td><strong>Seven addresses off one page.</strong> The Krivi Put baptisms for May–June
            1859 (<span class="mono">HDA 407, p.110</span>) hold <strong>seven Perpić children</strong>
            where this archive had recorded one, every household entered
            <em>Confiniarii</em>. The gazetteer above, built from the export, knows Krivi Put and
            Mrzli Dol but none of these numbers.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Mrzli Dol kbr. 53</strong><br /><span class="dim">župa Krivi Put</span></td>
          <td><strong>Prpić</strong></td>
          <td>Lucija Prpić's marriage, 18 May 1913 — <em>poljodjelci iz Mrzlog Dola</em>. The table
            above has only house 34 at Mrzli Dol. Her parents are the same Petar Prpić and Tonka
            Pavelić who fathered <strong>Mile</strong>, who married Franjka Papić in 1914: the two
            Papić–Prpić marriages in this archive join the same two households twice.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">Neither can be added to the counts above without inventing a
    record, because the tool reads the export and these are not in it. They are set down here
    instead, as the two places to look next. <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Open questions →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>How this was produced</h2>
    <p class="note"><code class="mono">tools/gazetteer.py</code> reduces every place string to a
    village and, where the register gives one, a house number. It ran on Žubrinić alone until
    September 2026; it now runs on all four surnames, which is what makes the shapes below
    comparable. It also folds the tree's spellings of one village together — <em>Otocac
    Croatia</em> with <em>Otočac</em>, <em>Kriviput</em> with <em>Krivi Put</em> — without which a
    village looks half as populated as it is.
    <code class="mono">tools/name_reuse.py</code> finds repeated given names among siblings, folding
    the Latin and Croatian forms of a name together (<em>Joannes = Ivan</em>,
    <em>Georgius = Juraj</em>), excluding the tree's <strong>sixteen sorting buckets</strong>, and
    separating three cases: reuse after a gap, the same year twice, and a gap too wide for one
    mother. Both write their output to <code class="mono">notes/</code>.
    <a href={u("/method")}>Method →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
